 "rule": {
  "ages": {
   "middle_from": 36,
   "old_from": 60,
   "child_under": 13
  },
  "lines": {
   "young": 1,
   "middle": 0.6,
   "old": 0.3
  },
  "younger_likelier": 0.5,
  "nearer_tone_likelier": 1.0,
  "never_darker_than": 1.0,
  "light_only_for": [
   "fair",
   "light",
   "ruddy"
  ],
  "seed": "chicago4d:face-library:v1:",
  "basis": "Who may wear which, all of it ours. A person takes a face of their own sex, and of their age's third (young under 36, middle-aged under 60, old from there) or of a younger one, each third younger half as likely: a young atlas on an older person is what every body wore before, the lines of age drawn over it. Nobody takes a face older than they are. A child under 13 takes a young woman's, which is smooth. Among those, a face whose own measured tone is nearer the person's is likelier (the person's tone is the skin most bodies of their sex wear, `texture.person_tone`, under their recorded complexion: a man's or a boy's is the men's, everybody else's the women's; it is not the skin of the body they happen to be drawn on, so a person drawn on two bodies of different skins wears one face on both), by a bell one doubling of light wide; a face more than a doubling darker than the person is never taken. A freckled face (`light_only`) is only for a person whose recorded complexion is fair, light or ruddy (`light_only_for`); nobody of another complexion, or with none recorded, takes one. The body's own skin stands in the draw as one more face. The pick is seeded by the person's id, so it is the same on every visit. It reads nothing of the body's skin; only the gain that brings the face to the skin around it is the body's own. The person's sex and age are their record's, and the body's where the record has none (on 2026-11-15, 465 of the 1,212 people drawn have no sex recorded and 131 neither an age nor a span of ages; a body for either sex, a toddler's, is read as a woman's): such a person wears one face on every body because their records draw them on bodies of one sex and size, which tools/test_human_variation.mjs holds over every row, and not by this rule. `lines`: how much of the drawn age lines (human-skin.js) a face keeps, by the face's own age, the middle-aged and old atlases having painted their own."
 },
 "left_out": {
  "basis": "The three packs hold 59 skins by their own records (kept beside the cuts as <pack>.original.json) and 22 are kept. What each of the other 37 was left out for, by our own look at its face (2026-11-14) except where a row says it was not looked at. The seven darkest were cut and tried and are the ones to come back for: brought to the tone of the bodies' skin and laid under the app's complexions their faces came out lighter and yellower than the neck beside them, with pale lips, the dark stubble painted round each face having entered the measure of where face meets skin. So the library holds no face painted for a dark skin yet: a person of dark complexion wears the same faces as everyone else under their own complexion, as they wore the bodies' one skin before. That is a gap in the library, not a ruling about anybody.",
